ii) power/amplitude of the fast spectral band, (iii) torsional autocorrelation time, and (iv) the distribution of dihedral increments per time step \u2014 within tolerance (below) and significantly better than non-learned baselines, while (v) *not inventing* fast oscillation on features that are genuinely slow/rigid.\n\n**Proposed acceptance thresholds (labeled assumptions, objectively anchored in \u00a77):**\n- Dominant frequency \u03bd_max in the fast band: median relative error \u2264 **10%**.\n- Fast-band spectral power P_B: median relative error \u2264 **20%**.\n- Torsional correlation time \u03c4_c: median relative error \u2264 **30%**.\n- Distributional distance (MMD on increments; KLD on smoothed increment histograms): within the **95th percentile of the gold-standard self-split reference** (the agreement achievable by a perfect model, defined in \u00a76).\n- Statistical significance over baselines: paired permutation test, **\u03b1 = 0.05**, on the primary distributional metrics.\n- Negative-control ceiling: generated fast-band power on a rigid dihedral \u2264 the gold-standard rigid-dihedral band power (\u2264 5% of the flexible feature's band power).\n- Thresholds pass on **\u2265 2 of 3 subsample strides and both systems** for a global \"accurate\" verdict.\n\n---\n\n## 2. System, force field, solvent, and MD setup [PROPOSED unless noted]\n\n**Systems (two, for robustness).**\n- **System A (primary, cheap):** alanine dipeptide (Ace\u2013Ala\u2013Nme) \u2014 two well-characterized backbone dihedrals (\u03c6, \u03c8) with known librational dynamics; low cost permits many replicates and sampling seeds.\n- **System B (secondary, more realistic):** trialanine (Ala\u2083) in explicit water, adding a second rotatable bond and intra-peptide coupling, confirming that results are not an artefact of a single-dihedral toy.\n\n**Force field / solvent [PROPOSED].** Amber ff14SB with TIP3P explicit water (chosen for protein compatibility with typical MDGen training data; an MDGen-specific training force field, if any, would be preferred \u2014 see \u00a78). Van der Waals cutoffs, PME electrostatics, and standard equilibration (energy minimization \u2192 NVT \u2192 NPT) as implemented in the chosen engine.\n\n**Integration.** Velocity Verlet / leapfrog, **2 fs** timestep with LINCS/SHAKE constraints on bonds to hydrogen (standard choice; constraint on fast bond-stretch modes leaves torsional modes as the dominant unresolved fast dynamics \u2014 consistent with the question's focus on torsion oscillations).\n\n**Save cadence (full resolution).** Save coordinates every **0.25 ps** (every 125 steps). This samples ~4 THz, comfortably above the torsional libration band (illustratively ~0.2\u20135 THz; the exact band is defined empirically in \u00a75, not by fiat), so the full-resolution trajectory contains the fast dynamics of interest.\n\n**Trajectory length.** ~20 ns per replicate [PROPOSED]: long enough for several slow-mode decorrelations (conformational transitions) while keeping MD cost low; a pilot run will confirm stability of the ensemble statistics (below).\n\n**Replicates.** **N = 10** independent full-resolution runs sharing the same initial equilibrated configuration but independent initial velocities [PROPOSED power choice; see \u00a76 pilot].\n\n---\n\n## 3. Subsampling scheme tied to the torsional band and the Nyquist limit\n\n**Full-resolution reference cadence:** \u0394t_ref = 0.25 ps \u21d2 sampling rate 4 ps\u207b\u00b9 \u21d2 Nyquist \u03bd_Nyq(ref) = 2 THz.\n\n**Subsample strides (independent variable):** keep every K-th frame of the full-resolution trajectory:\n- **K = 4** \u21d2 coarse cadence 1 ps \u21d2 \u03bd_Nyq = 0.5 THz \u21d2 removes oscillation periods < 2 ps.\n- **K = 8** \u21d2 coarse cadence 2 ps \u21d2 \u03bd_Nyq = 0.25 THz \u21d2 removes periods < 4 ps.\n- **K = 16** \u21d2 coarse cadence 4 ps \u21d2 \u03bd_Nyq \u2248 0.125 THz \u21d2 removes periods < ~8 ps.\n\n**Nyquist justification (fixed argument).** The torsional fast band B (empirically identified in \u00a75) is chosen so that B lies above the coarse Nyquist limit for each stride. Any spectral power in B present in the coarse trajectory is then either aliased or absent, i.e., the fast dynamics are *genuinely missing* from the input, not merely compressed. This is verified a priori by (a) computing the full-resolution PSD and locating B, and (b) confirming that a naive interpolation of the coarse trajectory has near-zero coherent power in B. The stride sweep (K = 4, 8, 16) tests monotonic sensitivity: larger K removes more of the fast band and should make recovery harder \u2014 a built-in dose\u2013response check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. MDGen usage: conditioning, seeds, and replicates\n\n**Conditioning interface.** Per the MDGen modeling paradigm [PROPOSED, pending source confirmation], MDGen is a latent diffusion model over MD trajectory frames with a conditional/super-resolution pathway: the model is given the **coarse anchor frames (Cartesian coordinates at subsampled time points) as conditioning context** and generates the omitted intermediate frames at full temporal resolution. Concretely, the design specifies:\n- Input: a coarse trajectory of anchor conformations (per stride K).\n- Output: generated completion at \u0394t = 0.25 ps spacing between anchors.\n- The exact conditioning mechanism (frame concatenation vs. latent embedding; chunk/context-window length; autoregressive vs. joint generation) **cannot be verified from this workspace** and is carried as an uncertainty (\u00a78); the design uses the model's official conditional-generation entry point as distributed in the source repository.\n\n**Sampling budget [PROPOSED, documented power choices].**\n- Replicates: **N = 10** full-resolution MD runs per system.\n- Seeds: **M = 50** MDGen stochastic sampling seeds per coarse trajectory (one coarse trajectory per replicate\u2013stride combination).\n- Completions analyzed: N \u00d7 (K = 4, 8, 16) \u00d7 M = **1,500 generated completions per system** (both Tier-2 pointwise comparisons use only the M completions of the same replicate; all Tier-1 distributional comparisons pool across M and N).\n- **Statistical power justification (pilot).** Before full-scale sampling, run a small pilot (System A, K = 8, 3 replicates, 10 seeds) to check that (a) the standard error of the ensemble-estimated metrics (\u03bd_max, P_B, \u03c4_c) across replicates is < 10% of the mean, and (b) \u03c4_c estimates are stable over the trajectory length. N = 10 and M = 50 are chosen to exceed the pilot-determined minima by ~2\u20133\u00d7; if the pilot shows the required numbers to be lower, the budget is reduced accordingly [PROPOSED].\n\n---\n\n## 5. Two-tier reference / gold-standard definition\n\n**Tier 1 \u2014 Statistical gold standard (primary reference).** For a given anchor set (replicate r, stride K), the \"truth\" of the fast dynamics is the empirical distribution of the omitted frames **pooled across all N = 10 independent full-resolution replicates** (same initial configuration, different initial velocities). Because the replicates are identically initialized, their divergent omitted-frame content is exactly the *true ensemble of fast dynamics consistent with the fixed coarse anchors* \u2014 the well-defined, reproducible object a generative model should match.\n\n- Gold-standard objects per (r, K): the set of full-resolution frames between the anchors; derived per-frame dihedrals; dihedral increment distributions; PSD; autocorrelation function.\n\n**Tier 2 \u2014 Pointwise reference (secondary bound).** The actual omitted frames of replicate r itself are used only to compute per-frame reconstruction error (RMSE on dihedral features, heavy-atom RMSD). The design *predicts and will document* that this error is large and irreducible (chaos), which (a) validates the necessity of the Tier-1 definition and (b) guards against the alternative interpretation that \"recover\" meant frame-by-frame determinism.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Metrics and analysis plan with acceptance criteria\n\n**Feature encoding.** For each relevant dihedral (\u03c6, \u03c8 of each residue; \u03c9 and any \u03c7 as controls), compute angle \u03b8(t) and map to (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) pairs to avoid \u00b1\u03c0 wrapping artefacts; retain unwrapped \u03b8 for spectral analysis (unwrap with cumulative 2\u03c0 corrections on consecutive differences < \u03c0).\n\n**Analysis pipeline (identical for MDGen completions, gold-standard frames, and baselines).**\n1. **Band definition (not by fiat):** Welch PSD of full-resolution dihedral time series; define the fast band B = [\u03bd_lo, \u03bd_hi] as the contiguous frequency range containing the torsional librational peak(s), verifying by construction that B lies above the coarse Nyquist for each K (\u00a73). Also compute a rigid-band reference (\u03c9 dihedral) for the negative control.\n2. **Spectral metrics:** dominant frequency \u03bd_max within B (argmax of PSD) and integrated fast-band power P_B.\n3. **Distributional metrics:** (i) MMD with Gaussian kernel between MDGen-generated and gold-standard pooled increment distributions \u0394\u03b8(t); (ii) KLD between smoothed histograms (pseudocount regularization); (iii) autocorrelation function C(\u03c4) of (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) and correlation time \u03c4_c = \u222b\u2080^{first zero} C(\u03c4)d\u03c4.\n4. **Pointwise metrics (Tier-2 only):** per-frame RMSE on (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) and heavy-atom RMSD between each generated completion of replicate r and the actual omitted frames of replicate r; reported as an irreducible-accuracy bound, not an acceptance criterion.\n5. **Negative-control metrics:** fast-band power P_B on the rigid \u03c9 dihedral in generated completions.\n\n**Acceptance criteria (proposed thresholds from \u00a71), applied per (system, stride):**\n- Median relative error: \u03bd_max \u2264 10%; P_B \u2264 20%; \u03c4_c \u2264 30%.\n- MMD and KLD \u2264 95th percentile of the **gold-standard self-split reference** (see \u00a76 control: distributional metrics between non-overlapping replicate halves), so \"accurate\" is anchored to what a perfect model could achieve given sampling noise, rather than to arbitrary numbers.\n- MDGen must outperform spline and thermal-noise baselines (paired permutation test, \u03b1 = 0.05) on MMD and KLD.\n- Negative control: generated rigid-band power \u2264 gold-standard rigid-band power (and \u2264 5% of flexible-band power).\n- **Global verdict:** \"accurate recovery\" is declared only if the first three acceptance criteria hold on \u2265 2 of 3 strides and both systems.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Controls and ablations with expected outcomes\n\n| # | Control/Ablation | Protocol | Expected outcome (not measured) | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C1 | Spline interpolation | Linear and cubic spline on (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) or Cartesian coordinates between anchors | Near-zero coherent fast-band power; distributional metrics far outside tolerance | Confirms the fast dynamics are truly missing in the coarse input and that recovery is non-trivial; null expectation |\n| C2 | Anchor + thermal noise | Anchors perturbed by Gaussian noise, \u03c3 = per-atom thermal RMS fluctuation \u221a(kT/m), geometry-renormalized | Flat (white) spectrum, decorrelated increments (too broad), \u03c4_c \u226a gold standard; fails P_B and \u03c4_c criteria | Distinguishes \"random plausible frames\" from \"structurally correct dynamics\" |\n| C3 | MDGen unconditional ablation | MDGen sampling identical but without anchor conditioning | Plausible frames but temporally incoherent with anchors; \u03c4_c and spectral metrics fail | Attributing any recovery specifically to anchor conditioning |\n| C4 | Rigid-feature negative control | Same pipeline on rigid \u03c9 dihedral (and a locked ring dihedral in System B) | Generated fast-band power negligible (within gold-standard rigid-band power) | Checks MDGen does not *invent* fast oscillation on genuinely slow/rigid features (false-positive rate for fast dynamics) |\n| C5 | Harmonic/toy pipeline validation | Analytic harmonic oscillator on a torsion with known \u03bd; run entire spectral pipeline (detrend, Welch, peak-find) | Known frequency recovered within pipeline error floor (target < 2\u20135%) | Validates the instrumentation can resolve the \u00b110% frequency tolerance; sets the measurement error floor |\n| C6 | Gold-standard self-split reference | Split the N = 10 replicates into two halves; compute MMD/KLD/\u03c4_c agreement between halves | A reference band of \"agreement achievable by a perfect model\" purely from sampling noise | Objective anchoring of thresholds in \u00a76 instead of by fiat |\n| C7 | Stride dose\u2013response | Compare K = 4 vs 8 vs 16 | Monotonic degradation of recovery as the missing band widens, if recovery is real and tied to removed dynamics | Consistency check that the effect is governed by the subsampling band, not confounds |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Assumptions-and-uncertainties register\n\n**Fixed requirements (from the task; not negotiable).**\n- The experiment must answer whether MDGen *accurately recovers* fast timestep dynamics missing in subsampled MD trajectories.\n- Design-only deliverable; no claim that an experiment was run; \"accurately recovers\" must be operationalized with a defensible gold standard.\n\n**Proposed choices (assumptions; can be adjusted but are needed to make the design concrete).**\n- \"Accurately recovers\" = statistical/spectral fidelity of the fast-dynamics distribution, with pointwise error a secondary bound (\u00a71).\n- Numerical acceptance thresholds (10% / 20% / 30%, MMD/KLD \u2264 95th percentile of self-split) (\u00a71, \u00a76).\n- Systems: alanine dipeptide + trialanine; ff14SB/TIP3P (\u00a72).\n- Full-resolution save cadence 0.25 ps; strides K = 4/8/16; band defined empirically (\u00a72, \u00a73).\n- Power choices N = 10 replicates, M = 50 seeds, 20 ns runs, with pilot confirmation (\u00a74).\n- Two-tier gold standard: ensemble distribution across identically-initiated replicates as primary truth; omitted frames as secondary (\u00a75).\n\n**Uncertainties that cannot be resolved from the (empty) workspace input.**\n- **MDGen conditioning interface:** the exact API for anchor-conditioned super-resolution (frame concatenation vs. latent conditioning, context-window length, chunking, autoregressive vs. joint) must be confirmed from the official MDGen source/paper, which is absent here.\n- **MDGen asset availability and distribution shift:** whether official weights/hyperparameters are obtainable and whether the model's training distribution matches ff14SB/TIP3P protein systems of this design (if MDGen was trained on a different force-field family or dataset, results may reflect distribution shift rather than the model's recovery capability).\n- **Exact fast-band boundaries:** the torsional frequencies depend on the specific force field and molecule; B is defined empirically (\u00a76) but the final numbers will differ from the illustrative 0.2\u20135 THz.\n- **Sufficiency of dihedral-level metrics:** whether Cartesian/contact-map checks are additionally required to claim \"dynamics recovery\" for the full trajectory rather than only torsion features; the design reports heavy-atom RMSD pointwise but does not assert a Cartesian spectral criterion.\n- **Compute feasibility:** MDGen sampling is GPU-heavy (diffusion denoising steps per completion); no GPU exists in this workspace (see \u00a79), so execution is delegated to capable external hardware.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Feasibility and compute/hardware requirements\n\n**Constraint (verified environment):** this workspace has no GPU, no MD engine, and no MDGen assets; therefore the design is specified for execution on capable hardware (e.g., a single multi-GPU node or cloud VM), while all purely analytical/validation steps (C5 harmonic pipeline, C6 self-split statistics) are CPU-runnable.\n\n**Rough resource estimate [PROPOSED]:**\n- **MD:** OpenMM or GROMACS; 2 systems \u00d7 10 replicates \u00d7 20 ns at 2 fs \u2248 200 ns total \u2014 trivially cheap on CPU (minutes-to-hours per replicate on 8\u201316 cores); full-resolution save at 0.25 ps is small.\n- **MDGen inference:** 1,500 completions per system \u2248 3,000 sampling calls total; each call is a diffusion denoising run over up to a few hundred latent frames. This is the dominant cost; on a single A100-class GPU it is feasible in days, with the System A pilot (30 completions) run first to (i) confirm the sampling API and (ii) calibrate per-completion cost and the N/M budget.\n- **Analysis:** all spectral/distributional/permutation statistics are cheap (CPU, numpy/scipy); C5 validates the pipeline first so the error floor is known before any MDGen spending.\n\n**Execution order (design, not a ran experiment):** (1) C5 harmonic validation \u2192 (2) MD production for Systems A and B \u2192 (3) band definition + Nyquist verification \u2192 (4) MDGen pilot (System A, K = 8) for API and power calibration \u2192 (5) full MDGen sampling across strides/seeds \u2192 (6) all controls C1\u2013C4, C7 \u2192 (7) acceptance criteria evaluation and verdict.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (whose documents field reads 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'); a filesystem-wide search found no track.md, no MDGen source/weights, no datasets, and empty /opt, /data, /tmp. Environment: system Python 3.14.4, no ML/MD packages (no PyTorch/MD engines), no GPU \u2014 so all experimental execution is delegated to external capable hardware and no run is claimed. The design's operationalization of 'accurately recovers' as statistical/spectral fidelity (not per-frame determinism) is grounded in MDGen being a stochastic generative diffusion model and in the chaotic divergence of identically-seeded MD runs in the fast band; the Nyquist-based subsampling argument (stride K removes bands above 1/(2\u00b7K\u00b7dt_ref)) anchors the 'genuinely missing' premise; the two-tier gold standard (N identically-initiated replicates define the true conditional ensemble of fast dynamics; omitted frames serve only as a pointwise bound) and the control set (C1 spline, C2 thermal noise, C3 unconditional ablation, C4 rigid-feature negative control, C5 harmonic pipeline validation, C6 self-split reference, C7 stride dose-response) were carried over from the approved plan and elaborated with expected outcomes and acceptance criteria.", "assumptions": ["'Accurately recovers' is operationalized as statistical/spectral fidelity of the fast-dynamics distribution (frequency, power, correlation time, increment distribution), with per-frame pointwise error reported only as a secondary, irreducible bound.", "Acceptance thresholds are proposed as medians: dominant frequency error <=10%, fast-band power error <=20%, correlation-time error <=30%, MMD/KLD within the 95th percentile of a gold-standard self-split, and MDGen significantly better than baselines at alpha=0.05.", "Systems: alanine dipeptide (primary) and trialanine (secondary) in TIP3P with Amber ff14SB; 2 fs integration; full-resolution save at 0.25 ps; strides K=4/8/16; 20 ns replicates.", "Power choices: N=10 identically-initiated full-resolution replicates and M=50 MDGen sampling seeds per coarse trajectory, subject to pilot calibration (target SE <10% of mean and autocorrelation stability).", "The reference truth for fast dynamics is the cross-replicate ensemble distribution of omitted frames given identical anchors (Tier 1); the same-replicate omitted frames form only the Tier 2 pointwise bound.", "The fast spectral band is defined empirically from the full-resolution PSD, not from fixed frequency numbers.", "MDGen is used in its conditional/anchor-conditioned super-resolution mode as the intended usage for this question."], "uncertainties": ["The exact MDGen conditioning/super-resolution interface (frame concatenation vs latent conditioning, context-window length, autoregressive vs joint) cannot be verified because the workspace contains no MDGen source or weights; it must be confirmed from the official repository/paper.", "Whether official MDGen weights are obtainable and whether its training distribution matches the proposed ff14SB/TIP3P protein systems is unresolved; strong distribution shift could confound results.", "The exact torsional fast-band boundaries depend on force field and molecule; they are defined empirically in the design but their final values are unknown a priori.", "Whether dihedral-level metrics suffice or Cartesian/contact-map spectral criteria must also be reported to fully claim 'dynamics recovery' is an open choice.", "Compute feasibility in practice: MDGen diffusion sampling is GPU-bound and this environment has no GPU, so execution is delegated to external capable hardware with cost estimates being proposals, not verified."], "summary": "Finalize complete experiment design for MDGen fast-dynamics recovery verification"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-56434f0892604b82af34b316b371487f", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Finalize complete experiment design for MDGen fast-dynamics recovery verification", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
